meta {
  name: Open Lead Service Ticket
  type: http
  seq: 15
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/leads/{{leadId}}/service-ticket
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201 || res.getStatus() === 200) {
    bru.setVar("quoteTicketId", res.getBody().id);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("opens a Quote ticket linked to the lead", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.category).to.equal("Quote");
    expect(body.leadId).to.equal(bru.getEnvVar("leadId") || bru.getVar("leadId"));
    expect(body.ticketNumber).to.match(/^QTE-\d+$/);
  });

  test("opens as `open`, never resolved", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.status).to.equal("open");
    expect(body.resolvedAt).to.equal(null);
  });

  test("is status-locked", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().isStatusLocked).to.equal(true);
  });

  test("is assigned to the caller", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().assignedUserId).to.not.equal(null);
  });

  test("records an opening timeline entry", function () {
    const timeline = res.getBody().timeline;
    expect(timeline).to.have.length.of.at.least(1);
    expect(timeline[0].type).to.equal("created");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /leads/:id/service-ticket — open the lead's quote ticket

  Opens the CRM service ticket for a lead, or returns the one it already has.
  Called by the **Start Quote** dialog on the Household page once a lead is
  chosen, so a quote in flight is visible on the CSR's desk.

  - **Module:** `leads`
  - **Permission:** `leads:write`
  - **Data scope:** `LeadAccessService.loadOwnedLead` runs first, so another
    producer's lead is a `404` and no ticket is written.

  ## Path params
  | param | type   | notes                                               |
  |-------|--------|-----------------------------------------------------|
  | id    | string | Lead ObjectId. A malformed id is a `404`, not a `500`. |

  No body.

  ## Why this endpoint is under `/leads`

  The caller is the producer or CSR who pressed Start Quote. They hold
  `leads:write`; a producer holds **no** `crm_service:write` at all, so routing
  this through `POST /crm/service-tickets` would `403` the very people it exists
  for. The verb belongs to the lead ("open a ticket for this enquiry") and only
  the record it writes belongs to CRM — the mirror image of
  `QuoteRecapsService` writing to `leadModel`.

  `CreateServiceTicketDto` deliberately has **no** `leadId` field, which makes
  "a lead-linked ticket can only come from here" a schema-level invariant rather
  than a convention.

  ## Idempotency

  Safe to call on every Start Quote run, including when the user picks a lead
  from the list that was already quoted once. Two guards, because neither alone
  is enough:

  1. A read on `{agencyId, leadId}` catches the common case cheaply.
  2. The partial-unique `{agencyId, leadId}` index arbitrates two concurrent
     runs; on `E11000` the loser re-reads and returns the winner's ticket.

  So there is **one quote ticket per lead, ever** — a second call returns the
  same `id`, not a second ticket.

  ## The ticket it creates

  | field            | value                                                     |
  |------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|
  | `category`       | `Quote`                                                    |
  | `ticketNumber`   | `QTE-<n>`                                                  |
  | `status`         | `open`                                                     |
  | `priority`       | `medium`                                                   |
  | `assignedUserId` | **the caller** — under `own` data scope this is what puts the ticket on their desk at all |
  | `leadId`         | the lead                                                   |
  | `householdId`    | the lead's household, when it has one                      |
  | `clientName` / `phone` / `email` | from the household's primary contact, falling back to the lead's own fields |

  ## The status lock — the reason this ticket exists as its own kind

  A quote ticket has **no status of its own**. It is finished exactly when its
  lead is, and letting the two disagree — a ticket resolved under a live lead, a
  sold lead trailing an open ticket forever — is what the link prevents.

  - `PATCH /crm/service-tickets/:id/status` on this ticket returns **`400`**,
    for every status, for every role. The UI renders a locked badge instead of
    the picker; this is the server-side half of the same rule.
  - It resolves when the lead reaches **any terminal status** — `Sold`,
    `Closed`, `Converted`, `Lost`, `Not Qualified` — via
    `LeadTicketsService.resolveForLead`, fired from `PATCH /leads/:id` and from
    `POST /sold-deals`. The resolution appends a `system` timeline entry and
    starts the 7-day archive clock.

  Consequence worth knowing: a lead abandoned without being moved to a terminal
  status leaves its ticket in the queue with no way to clear it.

  ## Response `201`

  A full `ServiceTicketView` — the same shape `GET /crm/service-tickets/:id`
  returns, including the fields this feature added:

  ```json
  {
    "id": "665f...9b2",
    "ticketNumber": "QTE-104",
    "category": "Quote",
    "status": "open",
    "leadId": "665f...ac1",
    "isStatusLocked": true,
    "leadStatus": "New",
    "householdId": "665f...77c",
    "assignedRep": "Casey Rep",
    "resolvedAt": null,
    "timeline": [
      {
        "type": "created",
        "content": "Quote started. This ticket resolves when the lead is marked Sold or Closed."
      }
    ]
  }
  ```

  `leadStatus` is populated here and by the single-ticket read only. List
  responses leave it `null` — filling it there would cost one lead read per row
  for a field no list displays.

  ## Errors
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — `leads` module disabled, or missing `leads:write`.
  - `404 Not Found` — no such lead, a malformed id, or a lead outside scope.
}
